      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Terraform for AWS\nThe AWS provider lets Terraform\nmanage resources in Amazon Web\nServices through the AWS APIs.\nHashiCorp maintains it under the\nsource address `hashicorp/aws`\n([provider docs](https://registry.\nterraform.io/providers/hashicorp/a\nws/latest/docs)). A second\nprovider, `hashicorp/awscc`,\nreaches AWS through the Cloud\nControl API instead ([awscc docs](\nhttps://registry.terraform.io/prov\niders/hashicorp/awscc/latest/docs)\n). The [Terraform\nmap](../terraform.md) explains\nTerraform itself.\n\nThe unit is one AWS account and\none region. Each provider block\nreaches one account through a\nchain of identities, and it works\nin one region by default ([The\nIdentity Chain](#the-identity-chai\nn-the-identity-chain)). AWS has\nhundreds of services. A data\nengineer meets a few of them:\n\n- S3 (Simple Storage Service)\nbuckets hold data files, such as\nIceberg tables and files for\nSnowpipe.\n- IAM roles and policies decide\nwho may read and write those\nfiles. IAM is AWS Identity and\nAccess Management.\n- Secrets Manager holds passwords\nand keys for programs.\n- Queues in SQS (Simple Queue\nService), topics in SNS (Simple\nNotification Service) and Lambda\nfunctions react to new files.\n- Services in ECS (Elastic\nContainer Service) and databases\nin RDS (Relational Database\nService) run the apps that produce\nor serve data.\n\nFour ideas explain most AWS plans:\n\n- Many AWS settings are one\ndocument, such as the notification\nsettings of a bucket. Each\ndocument can have only one owner\nin Terraform ([One Document, One O\nwner](#one-document-one-owner-one-\ndocument-one-owner)).\n- Some resources add one item to a\nset, and others own the whole set\n([Adders and Owners](#adders-and-o\nwners-adders-and-owners)).\n- Access between AWS and another\nsystem needs trust on both sides\n([Trust Has Two Sides](#trust-has-\ntwo-sides-trust-has-two-sides)).\n- Every value that Terraform reads\nor writes, secrets included, sits\nin the state ([State Holds Secret \nValues](#state-holds-secret-values\n-state-holds-secret-values)).\n\nDeletes also differ by service,\nand each provider version changes\nsome rules ([Deletes Differ by Ser\nvice](#deletes-differ-by-service-d\neletes-differ-by-service);\n[Provider Generations](#provider-g\nenerations-provider-generations)).\n\nNow compare Snowflake. In\nSnowflake, the role that creates\nan object owns it, and grants\ndecide access. In AWS, the account\nowns every resource, and IAM\npolicies decide access. A\nSnowflake grant connects a role to\nan object; an IAM policy connects\nan identity to actions on\nresources.\n\nThe map has four parts. Mental\nModels explains the ideas above.\nReading an AWS Configuration says\nwhat you will see and where to\nlook first. Pitfalls lists the\nfailures that experienced users\nplan around. Good and Bad\nDecisions compares the main design\nchoices.",
      "fontSize": 18,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 22.5,
      "containerId": "node-7deeeb828571",
      "originalText": "Terraform for AWS\nThe AWS provider lets Terraform manage resources in Amazon Web Services through the AWS APIs. HashiCorp maintains it under the source address `hashicorp/aws` ([provider docs](https://registry.terraform.io/providers/hashicorp/aws/latest/docs)). A second provider, `hashicorp/awscc`, reaches AWS through the Cloud Control API instead ([awscc docs](https://registry.terraform.io/providers/hashicorp/awscc/latest/docs)). The [Terraform map](../terraform.md) explains Terraform itself.\n\nThe unit is one AWS account and one region. Each provider block reaches one account through a chain of identities, and it works in one region by default ([The Identity Chain](#the-identity-chain-the-identity-chain)). AWS has hundreds of services. A data engineer meets a few of them:\n\n- S3 (Simple Storage Service) buckets hold data files, such as Iceberg tables and files for Snowpipe.\n- IAM roles and policies decide who may read and write those files. IAM is AWS Identity and Access Management.\n- Secrets Manager holds passwords and keys for programs.\n- Queues in SQS (Simple Queue Service), topics in SNS (Simple Notification Service) and Lambda functions react to new files.\n- Services in ECS (Elastic Container Service) and databases in RDS (Relational Database Service) run the apps that produce or serve data.\n\nFour ideas explain most AWS plans:\n\n- Many AWS settings are one document, such as the notification settings of a bucket. Each document can have only one owner in Terraform ([One Document, One Owner](#one-document-one-owner-one-document-one-owner)).\n- Some resources add one item to a set, and others own the whole set ([Adders and Owners](#adders-and-owners-adders-and-owners)).\n- Access between AWS and another system needs trust on both sides ([Trust Has Two Sides](#trust-has-two-sides-trust-has-two-sides)).\n- Every value that Terraform reads or writes, secrets included, sits in the state ([State Holds Secret Values](#state-holds-secret-values-state-holds-secret-values)).\n\nDeletes also differ by service, and each provider version changes some rules ([Deletes Differ by Service](#deletes-differ-by-service-deletes-differ-by-service); [Provider Generations](#provider-generations-provider-generations)).\n\nNow compare Snowflake. In Snowflake, the role that creates an object owns it, and grants decide access. In AWS, the account owns every resource, and IAM policies decide access. A Snowflake grant connects a role to an object; an IAM policy connects an identity to actions on resources.\n\nThe map has four parts. Mental Models explains the ideas above. Reading an AWS Configuration says what you will see and where to look first. Pitfalls lists the failures that experienced users plan around. Good and Bad Decisions compares the main design choices.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "terraform-aws"
      }
    },
